\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 UPS\uc2  Reaches Agreement With Union To Raise Pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 1,391 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  faced a potential walkout by more than 325,000 union members after their five-year contract expires next week.\par \par \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  announced Tuesday that it had reached a tentative deal on a five-year contract with the union representing more than 325,000 of its U.S. workers, a key step in averting a potential strike.\par \par \uc2 The union, the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , reported in June that its \uc2 UPS\uc2  members had voted to authorize a walkout after the expiration of the current agreement on Aug. 1, with 97 percent of those who took part in the vote endorsing the move.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  handles about one-quarter of the tens of millions of packages that are shipped daily in the United States, and the strike prospect has threatened to dent economic activity, particularly the e-commerce industry.\par \par \uc2 Representatives from more than 150 Teamster locals will meet on Monday to review the agreement, and rank-and-file members will vote on it from Aug. 3 to Aug. 22, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations had broken down in early July, largely over the issue of part-time pay, before resuming Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 ''We demanded the best contract in the history of \uc2 UPS\uc2 , and we got it,'' the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president, Sean M. O'Brien, said in a statement. ''\uc2 UPS\uc2  has put $30 billion in new money on the table as a direct result of these negotiations.''\par \par \uc2 The company said it could not comment on the dollar value of the deal ahead of its second-quarter earnings call in early August.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  said that under the tentative agreement, current full- and part-time \uc2 UPS\uc2  employees represented by the union would receive a $2.75-an-hour raise this year, and $7.50 an hour in raises over the course of the contract.\par \par \uc2 The minimum pay for part-timers will rise to $21 an hour -- far above the current minimum starting pay of $16.20 -- and the top rate for full-time delivery drivers will rise to $49 an hour. Full-time drivers currently make $42 an hour on average after four years.\par \par \uc2 The company has also pledged to create 7,500 new full-time union jobs and to fill 22,500 open positions, for which part-time workers will be eligible. The company has said that part-time workers are essential to navigating bursts of activity over the course of a day and during busy months, and that many part-timers graduate to full-time jobs.\par \par \uc2 ''Together we reached a win-win-win agreement on the issues that are important to \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  leadership, our employees and to \uc2 UPS\uc2  and our customers,'' \uc2 Carol Tom\u233\'00\'E9\uc2 , the company's chief executive, said in a statement. ''This agreement continues to reward \uc2 UPS\uc2 's full- and part-time employees with industry-leading pay and benefits while retaining the flexibility we need to stay competitive.''\par \par \uc2 The union had cited the company's strong pandemic-era performance, with net adjusted income up more than 70 percent last year from 2019, as a reason that workers deserved substantial raises.\par \par \uc2 It had especially emphasized the need to improve pay for part-timers, who account for more than half the U.S. employees represented by the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , and who the union said earn ''near-minimum wage'' in many areas.\par \par \uc2 The path to the agreement appeared to be paved weeks ago after the two sides resolved what was arguably their most contentious issue, a new class of worker created under the previous contract.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  had said the arrangement was intended to allow workers to take on dual roles, like sorting packages some days and driving on other days, especially Saturdays, as a way to keep up with growing demand for weekend delivery.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  said that the hybrid idea was never actually carried out, and that in practice the new category of workers drove full time Tuesday through Saturday, only for less pay than other drivers. The company said that, under the previous contract, the Saturday drivers made about 87 percent of the base pay of other drivers and that some workers did work in a dual role.\par \par \uc2 Under the tentative agreement, the lower-paid category of drivers will be eliminated, and workers who drive Tuesday through Saturday will be converted to regular full-time drivers.\par \par \uc2 The deal also stipulates that no driver will be required to work an unscheduled sixth day in a week, which drivers had at times been forced to do under the existing contract to keep up with Saturday demand.\par \par \uc2 The two sides also agreed on several key noneconomic issues, such as heat safety. Under the proposed deal, new trucks must have air-conditioning beginning in January, while existing trucks will be outfitted with additional fans and venting.\par \par \uc2 Whether it passes will partly be a political test for Mr. O'Brien, who was elected to head the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  in 2021 while regularly criticizing his predecessor, \uc2 James P. Hoffa\uc2 , as being too accommodating toward employers and toward \uc2 UPS\uc2  in particular.\par \par \uc2 Mr. O'Brien argued that Mr. Hoffa had effectively forced \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers to accept a deeply flawed contract in 2018, even after they voted it down, and accused his Hoffa-backed rival of being reluctant to strike against the company.\par \par \uc2 Since taking over as president last year, he has frequently said the union would be aggressive in pressuring \uc2 UPS\uc2  and suggested on several occasions that a strike was likely.\par \par \uc2 A few days before the agreement on eliminating the hybrid worker position, Mr. O'Brien said in a statement that the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  were walking away from the table over an ''appalling counterproposal'' and that a strike ''now appears inevitable.''\par \par \uc2 The company sought to reassure customers and the public that a deal would be consummated despite the occasionally heated pronouncements.\par \par \uc2 On an earnings call in April, the \uc2 UPS\uc2  chief executive, Ms. Tom\u233\'00\'E9, said that the two sides were aligned on many key issues and that outsiders should not be distracted by the ''great deal of noise'' that was likely to arise in the run-up to a deal.\par \par \uc2 The deal, if ratified, removes a serious threat to the U.S. economy. Economists say a strike by \uc2 UPS\uc2  employees would have made it harder for businesses to ship goods on time, and the resulting restrictions in supply chains would probably have stoked inflation just as it had shown signs of easing.\par \par \uc2 ''It would have been devastating to the economy, just given the size and scale of \uc2 UPS\uc2 ,'' said Mike Skordeles, head of U.S. economics at Truist Advisory Services. ''You can't just pull out a player that big without causing disruption and prices to go up.''\par \par \uc2 A 10-day UPS strike would cost the U.S. economy about $7 billion, according to an estimate from the Anderson Economic Group.\par \par \uc2 Small businesses were most at risk from a strike as \uc2 UPS\uc2  might be their sole or primary shipping provider, meaning they would have to scramble for alternatives. Large retailers tend to have more diversified delivery providers and are more likely to have contingency plans to soften the blow.\par \par \uc2 Mr. O'Brien had explicitly asked President Biden, who has called himself ''the most pro-labor union president,'' not to get involved in the negotiations. A group of over two dozen Democratic senators also pledged not to intervene.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration helped broker a deal that headed off a freight rail strike last year. Many union members involved in that dispute saw the deal as leaning too heavily in favor of the major rail carriers.\par \par \uc2 In 1997, about 185,000 \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers staged a strike for 15 days. That time, the company reported that the strike cost it more than $600 million. But the last strike happened when e-commerce was in its infancy. \uc2 UPS\uc2  has benefited from the e-commerce boom: In 2022 it reported more than $100 billion in revenue, compared with $31 billion in 2002.\par \par \uc2 J. Edward Moreno and Peter Eavis contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 J. Edward Moreno and Peter Eavis contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Union representatives will meet on Monday to review the agreement, and rank-and-file members will vote from Aug. 3 to Aug. 22 to formally ratify the deal. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MAANSI SRIVASTAVA/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230726ej7q0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
