\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 With writers on strike (and actors possibly right behind them), it's an awkward time for Emmy nominations.\b0\par\par\uc2 By John Koblin \par \uc2 325 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The normally joyous affair of toasting television's best is happening while much of Hollywood is shut down.\par \par \uc2 More than 11,000 television and feature film writers have been on strike for 72 days. The union representing 160,000 actors has been in negotiations with the major Hollywood studios for over a month -- and could declare a strike as soon as its contract expires late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 So, who's ready to party?\par \par \uc2 The timing of the Emmy nominations this year is awkward, to say the least. With thousands out of work, a normally joyous affair toasting the best in the television business is happening while much of Hollywood is shut down, and the mood is decidedly grim.\par \par \uc2 The writers and the studios have not returned to the bargaining table since negotiations collapsed in early May. They remain far apart on the issues, which means the Emmys ceremony, scheduled for Sept. 18, may very well be postponed.\par \par \uc2 The Television Academy, which administers the Emmys, and Fox, this year's broadcaster, have not made their plans public. But organizers have privately concluded that if the strike -- or, worse, a pair of them -- continues for another two or three weeks, they will have to postpone the September event, probably by months. The ceremony could be moved to as late as January.\par \par \uc2 The writers and actors have similar grievances. Both argue that their compensation levels, and their working conditions, have deteriorated during the streaming era. On the other side, major entertainment companies have watched their profit levels collapse as the old cable model comes undone.\par \par \uc2 With thousands of writers out of work, the mood around a normally joyous affair toasting the best in the television business is decidedly grim. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Francesca Forquet for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230713ej7d0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
