\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Actors Union Could Soon Join Writers on a Strike If a Deal Is Not Reached\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Sperling \par \uc2 625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News: Actors could join writers on the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 The actors may soon be joining Hollywood screenwriters on the picket lines if their union, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , and the major studios fail to reach a deal by midnight on Wednesday. The two sides are haggling over the same issues that are front and center for the Writers Guild of America: higher wages, increased residual payments (a type of royalty) and significant guardrails around the use of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Should the actors go on strike, it will be the first time in 63 years that both the actors and the writers are out at the same time over a contract dispute.\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters: A second strike could shut Hollywood down completely.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood is already 80 percent shut down since the writers went on strike on May 2. While some television shows and movies continued filming, the writers were surprisingly effective in shutting down shows in production. If the actors join them on the picket lines, productions will be closed completely, a reality that will have a significant effect on the local economies in Los Angeles and other filming locales like Atlanta and New York City. During the last writers' strike 15 years ago, the Los Angeles economy lost an estimated $2.1 billion.\par \par \uc2 The effects of a dual strike would also soon be coming to your television, with network shows going into reruns and a likely proliferation of reality television. Also, actors would no longer be able to promote new films, a reality that already exists to a large degree because the writers' strike forced the late-night shows to go dark.\par \par \uc2 Background: Streaming and A.I. bring change.\par \par \uc2 Not since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was the president of the \uc2 Screen Actors Guild\uc2  have the writers and actors been on strike at the same time. Back then, the actors were fighting over residuals paid for licensing films for television. Today, the actors want to ensure higher wages and better residuals in an entertainment landscape in which studios are struggling to turn a profit after investing billions of dollars in streaming. The actors are also concerned about how their likenesses could be used with the advent of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Guild members authorized the strike in early June, with 97.9 percent of members voting yes. Then on June 24, Fran Drescher, the president of \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , and Duncan Crabtree-Ireland, the national executive director of the guild, informed its membership that they ''remained optimistic'' about the talks. They added that the negotiations with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , the trade association negotiating for the studios, had been ''extremely productive.''\par \par \uc2 A video prompted a group of more than 1,000 actors, including Ms. Drescher, to sign a letter that urged the union's leadership to not settle for a lesser deal. ''We are prepared to strike,'' the letter said.\par \par \uc2 On June 30, the union announced that it had extended its contract until Wednesday while the sides continued to talk.\par \par \uc2 What's Next: Could a deal still happen?\par \par \uc2 After the parties negotiated all weekend, it remained unclear whether they were any closer to a resolution. Should they fail to make an agreement by midnight Pacific time on Wednesday, some 160,000 \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members will be poised to join the 11,000 writers already on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 If a deal isn't reached by Wednesday, it would be the first time in 63 years that writers and actors were striking at the same time over contract disputes. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ROBYN BECK/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230711ej7b0004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
