\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Looming Strike Spurs Supply Chain Woes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jordyn Holman and J. Edward Moreno \par \uc2 1,214 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Businesses around the country are facing what could be the latest disruption to how they get their goods to their customers in a timely and affordable fashion.\par \par \uc2 Kathryn Keeler and her husband, Stuart de Haaff, own an olive oil company in the hills of central California. The couple spend their days harvesting olives, bottling the oil, labeling the glass bottles and shipping them out, relying primarily on \uc2 UPS\uc2  to get their product to kitchens throughout the United States.\par \par \uc2 They are far from alone. \uc2 UPS\uc2  handles about a fourth of packages shipped each day in the United States, according to the Pitney Bowes Parcel Shipping Index, many of them for small businesses like Ms. Keeler's company, Rancho Azul y Oro.\par \par \uc2 But with the labor contract between \uc2 UPS\uc2  and 325,000 of its workers expiring at the end of the month and a potential strike looming, business owners around the country are facing what could be the latest in a series of supply chain disruptions they have confronted since the start of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Some are pre-emptively turning to \uc2 FedEx\uc2 , the next largest private carrier in the United States, or the Postal Service. Others are calling their third-party shippers -- firms that work with the likes of \uc2 UPS\uc2 , \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and \uc2 DHL\uc2  to handle their clients' shipping needs -- to ensure that their packages can still get to their final destinations even if there is a strike.\par \par \uc2 The logistical challenge is just one more burden on businesses that have been stretched thin over the past few years.\par \par \uc2 ''Maybe a larger business can withstand those types of situations,'' Ms. Keeler said. But as small-business owners, she and her husband ''don't have a lot of extra time in our day to be on the phone with the post office or \uc2 FedEx\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Since 2020, the pandemic has strained the global supply chain in a number of ways. E-commerce reached record levels as stuck-at-home Americans bought clothes, furniture, workout equipment and groceries online. Companies had to navigate Covid-related shutdowns at factories in China and Vietnam. There were worldwide delays when a large container ship got stuck in the Suez Canal, leading to containers piling up at the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 . Those situations affected the way goods came into the United States.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 UPS\uc2  strike could hobble the way brands move their wares domestically.\par \par \uc2 ''This is something that affects us on our home turf, and how do we solve for that?'' said Ron Robinson, the chief executive of BeautyStat Cosmetics, which uses \uc2 UPS\uc2  to ship its skin care products to retailers like Ulta and \uc2 Macy's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One strategy that his team will lean on is trying to bundle packages, sending as many as it can out at once, he said.\par \par \uc2 Switching to another carrier is going to cost some companies.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Culver, the chief executive of Platterful, a monthly charcuterie board subscription service, also uses \uc2 UPS\uc2 . Switching over to \uc2 FedEx Express\uc2  -- necessary to ensure that the meats in his packages reach consumers in time -- would cost about $5 to $10 more per delivery.\par \par \uc2 Teri Johnson, the founder of Harlem Candle Company, received an email on June 26 from her third-party shipper about a potential \uc2 UPS\uc2  strike. It suggested she switch to \uc2 FedEx\uc2 . That will cost her about $2 extra for each candle shipped in the greater New York area. Sending her candles to California will cost even more.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't really have a choice right now,'' Ms. Johnson said.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  said it was accepting additional volume for a limited time and would assess how much capacity its network could accommodate. ''Shippers who are considering shifting volume to \uc2 FedEx\uc2 , or are currently in discussions with the company to open a new account, are encouraged to begin shipping with \uc2 FedEx\uc2  now,'' the company said in a post on its website on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Postal Service said in an emailed statement that it ''has a strong network, and we have the capacity to deliver what is tendered to us.''\par \par \uc2 Larger companies are relying on sophisticated backup plans that have been tested over the past few years. The pandemic and previous tariff trade wars pushed many major retailers with global supply chains to diversify the countries where their vendors are and the parcel carriers they use.\par \par \uc2 ''We've been focused on investing in a lot of transportation solutions that allow us to more nimbly move freight between carriers,'' said Alexis DePree, the chief supply chain officer at \uc2 Nordstrom\uc2 . ''We can do that with a lot more flexibility and speed than we were able to in the past.''\par \par \uc2 Some third-party carriers are seeing a boost in their businesses as the possibility of a \uc2 UPS\uc2  strike comes into focus for their clients. Stord, a third-party logistics and technology provider based in Atlanta whose clients include apparel makers and consumer-package companies, has been sending emails out telling its clients not to worry. Stord uses a cloud-based platform to offer services like warehousing and fulfillment and handles tens of thousands of their packages a day.\par \par \uc2 By combining the volume of its broad portfolio of client brands and using software to make decisions, Stord has the leverage to better negotiate prices with the large parcel carriers, said Sean Henry, the company's chief executive.\par \par \uc2 ''We've been negotiating with \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and U.S.P.S. about rates around \uc2 UPS\uc2  so our customers don't have to do that,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Stord said more of its clients had asked it to negotiate with carriers on their behalf. He said that equated to ''tens of millions of dollars of annual revenue'' for his business.\par \par \uc2 Still, some business owners are not letting the possibility of a \uc2 UPS\uc2  strike stress them out just yet.\par \par \uc2 Bill McHenry, president of Widgeteer, which sells cookware to large retailers, said he felt ''kind of numb'' after navigating the pandemic-related challenges. ''I've seen a lot of stuff and the stories that I've heard and things we've had to go through and survive -- not just the pricing but the upheaval of thinking you have a container but don't,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 He said the potential rail strike last December had been a bigger concern for him.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the possibility that a deal could be reached between \uc2 UPS\uc2  and the union that represents its workers, the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , remains. The union announced on Wednesday that negotiations had broken down, after previously saying the sides had reached a tentative agreement. If an agreement is not reached, a strike could happen as early as Aug. 1.\par \par \uc2 If that occurs, ''we would be collateral damage,'' Ms. Keeler said.\par \par \uc2 UPS handles about 25 percent of the packages shipped in the United States. A strike could hobble the way brands move their wares domestically (PHOTOGRAPH BY DESIREE RIOS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B1); With the possibility of a UPS strike starting as early as Aug. 1, some business owners are pre-emptively turning to other carriers, such as FedEx or the U.S. Postal Service. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JOE RAEDLE/GETTY IMAGES; HUNTER KERHART FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B1, B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230709ej780002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
