\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Latest Jobs Data Sends Wall St. in a Spin\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 655 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wall Street fell Thursday after reports suggested the job market remained much more resilient than expected.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 lost 0.8 percent. The Dow Jones industrial average dropped 1.1 percent, and the Nasdaq composite gave up 0.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 While a sturdy labor market keeps the economy out of a long-feared recession, it could also push the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to keep interest rates higher for longer in its campaign to defeat high inflation. That in turn could mean more pressure down the line on the economy and financial markets.\par \par \uc2 A report from ADP Research Institute suggested hiring by private employers was much stronger last month than economists expected, with nearly twice as many jobs created than forecast.\par \par \uc2 The ADP report can be volatile and ''isn't necessarily a good predictor of the monthly jobs report'' that is more comprehensive and due from the government on Friday, said \uc2 Mike Loewengart\uc2 , head of model portfolio construction at Morgan Stanley Global Investment Office.\par \par \uc2 But it also paired with a separate report showing the number of workers applying for unemployment last week remained low relative to history, even if it was a bit higher than expected.\par \par \uc2 Other reports on Thursday offered a nuanced picture. One said employers advertised fewer job openings in May than expected. That could mean less upward pressure on inflation. A separate report meanwhile said growth in U.S. services industries remained hot and accelerated in June.\par \par \uc2 If the Friday jobs report is strong like the ADP report, it could mean counterintuitive pain for stocks because it would push the Fed to keep the brakes on the economy in hopes of getting inflation under wraps.\par \par \uc2 ''Whether it's that big of a number'' as what the ADP report suggested ''or even half of that, it would still be showing that the labor market is very strong and the Fed has not done enough to get inflation down,'' said Megan Horneman, chief investment officer at Verdence Capital Advisors.\par \par \uc2 ''Even with some of this nuanced economic data, the bottom line is the labor market is always a lagging indicator'' and later to crack under the weight of higher interest rates than other parts of the economy, she said. ''We still expect the labor market to get weaker.''\par \par \uc2 She expects a recession to hit within the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 Yields jumped in the bond market as traders ramped up bets for the Fed to keep rates higher for longer than previously expected. Hopes for a potential cut to interest rates by early next year diminished.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year Treasury rose to 4.04 percent from 3.94 percent late Wednesday. It helps set rates for mortgages and other important loans.\par \par \uc2 The two-year Treasury yield, which moves more on expectations for the Fed, climbed to 4.99 percent from 4.95 percent, back to where it was in early March, before the failures of several banks rattled confidence across financial markets.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  was one of the heaviest weights on the market after it tumbled 3.7 percent. It warned of a hit to its profit during the spring because of changes in gas prices and industry margins, among other items.\par \par \uc2 JetBlue Airways\uc2  sank 7.2 percent after it said it will end a partnership with \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  in the northeastern United States after losing a court fight over the deal. \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  will focus instead on salvaging its proposed purchase of \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2 . \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  fell 2.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp, wavered between small gains and losses after unveiling its new app Threads, a rival to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , which has had a bumpy ride under new owner \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . \uc2 Meta\uc2  ended the day down 0.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230707ej7700044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
