\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Labor Market Is Cooling Off, But Millions of Jobs Remain\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. Edward Moreno \par \uc2 637 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 Job openings fell in May while the number of workers quitting their jobs increased, the Labor Department reported Thursday.\par \par \uc2 There were 9.8 million job openings in May, down from 10.3 million in April, according to the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey, known as JOLTS. The report shows that the labor market is maintaining ample opportunities for workers, but that it is losing momentum.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a labor market that is moderating, where things are cooling down, but is still hot,'' said \uc2 Nick Bunker\uc2 , the director of North American economic research at the job search website Indeed.\par \par \uc2 The quits rate, which is often used to gauge a worker's confidence in the job market, increased in May, particularly in the health care, social assistance and construction industries. A rise in quitting often signals workers' confidence that they will be able to find other work, often better paying. But fewer workers are quitting their jobs than were doing so last year at the height of what was called the ''great resignation.''\par \par \uc2 Layoffs were relatively steady after decreasing in previous months, a sign that employers are hesitant to let go of workers.\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters: The Fed's next move on interest rates is unclear.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  have worried about the strength of the labor market as they continue to tackle stubbornly high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Fed chose to leave interest rates unchanged in its June meeting after 10 consecutive increases. The JOLTS report is one of several factors that will inform the Fed's next decision on rates.\par \par \uc2 Some economists worry that the Fed will push interest rates too high and set off a recession.\par \par \uc2 But the JOLTS report as well as previous economic temperature checks have led others to believe that a ''soft landing'' -- an outcome in which inflation eases to the Fed's goal of 2 percent without a recession -- is within reach. The biggest question is whether wage growth can continue to cool as workers switch jobs, said \uc2 Aaron Terrazas\uc2 , chief economist at the career site \uc2 Glassdoor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''A tight labor market does not necessarily have to be inflationary,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Background: A cooling labor market retains underlying strength.\par \par \uc2 The labor market has remained resilient amid the Fed's efforts to slow down the economy but has shown signs of cooling in recent months. Job openings were down for three consecutive months until April.\par \par \uc2 Initial jobless claims during the week that ended Saturday, also released by the Labor Department on Thursday, nudged higher from the week before, though the four-week trend shows initial claims declining.\par \par \uc2 Although job openings are cooling, the reading of 9.8 million in May is high compared with prepandemic levels. In 2019, for example, the monthly totals hovered around seven million.\par \par \uc2 ''To some degree, I worry we've become desensitized to numbers that were once upon a time eye-popping,'' Mr. Terrazas said.\par \par \uc2 What's Next: The June jobs report comes Friday.\par \par \uc2 The June employment report -- another indicator closely watched by the Fed -- will be released by the Labor Department on Friday. Economists surveyed by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  expect the report to show a gain of 225,000, down from the initial reading of 339,000 for May.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate jumped to 3.7 percent in May, from 3.4 percent a month earlier. Although still historically low, the rate was the highest since October and exceeded analysts' expectations.\par \par \uc2 Fed policymakers will hold their next meeting July 25-26.\par \par \uc2 College students waiting to speak with representatives of tech companies at a job fair in Atlanta. Many employers remain hesitant to let go of workers. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALEX SLITZ/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230707ej770003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
