\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Shifting Its Business Model, \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  Says It Will Let Drivers Opt for Hourly Pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kellen Browning \par \uc2 676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The company said the option would give couriers greater choice. It could also help \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  find people who will make less desirable deliveries.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  said on Wednesday that it would begin giving its delivery drivers the option to be paid an hourly minimum wage, instead of earning money for each delivery.\par \par \uc2 The significant shift in compensation could be an answer to concerns that some delivery people are not paid fairly. It could also add an incentive for drivers to pick up smaller orders that don't pay as well and that they would typically avoid.\par \par \uc2 Drivers will be able to choose whether they earn money for each order -- usually a few dollars in base pay plus compensation for miles driven -- or receive a flat hourly amount, \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The hourly rate includes only active time, meaning time between accepting and dropping off an order, and does not include the period when drivers are waiting for the next order. Drivers will be able to toggle between the two payment methods. Tips would be applied on top of the hourly base pay, the company said.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , which uses gig workers to transport food and other deliveries, announced the change as part of Dash Forward, a product event marking \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 's 10th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  said it was adding the payment option in response to driver feedback, and because it wanted to give drivers more decision-making power.\par \par \uc2 ''One of the things we've heard a lot is around choice: Choice of when, where and how they earn is really important,'' said Cody Aughney, head of the company's Dasher & Logistics team.\par \par \uc2 The relationship between gig workers and companies like \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and \uc2 Uber\uc2  has been scrutinized in recent years by regulators and labor activists. The biggest questions have been over how those workers are classified and whether they are adequately paid.\par \par \uc2 Gig drivers are usually independent contractors who are responsible for their own expenses and do not receive benefits like full-time employees. They have long complained that they are underpaid and sometimes exploited by the companies.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  said drivers who chose to be paid hourly and those earning money per delivery were likely to earn a similar amount. The minimum compensation will depend on the region and range from $10 to $19.50 per hour, the company said.\par \par \uc2 The new payment method is similar to Proposition 22, a 2020 California ballot measure that was backed by gig companies and guaranteed drivers a minimum wage and other limited benefits in exchange for precluding them from being classified as employees.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  said there was a big difference: Drivers can switch between hourly and per-delivery pay as frequently as they want. The new system will not be used in California, Seattle or New York -- areas that have passed laws governing minimum pay for drivers.\par \par \uc2 Sergio Avedian, a longtime driver and a contributor to The Rideshare Guy, a blog that provides tips to gig drivers, said an hourly pay option ''gives the drivers a little bit of a comfort zone.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Avedian, who encourages drivers to decline orders that are unlikely to offer a decent payday or a good tip, said the hourly payment could be a way for \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  to get them to accept smaller deliveries they would have skipped.\par \par \uc2 ''On their end, the point is to push as many orders as they can, and on the driver's end, it may give them some security,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Because some drivers do decline less desirable orders, \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  said, those who accept everything they are offered receive a disproportionate number of those cheaper deliveries and are put at a disadvantage. Hourly minimum pay, the company said, will help that group.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash's new compensation system will not be used in California, Seattle or New York, which have passed laws governing minimum pay for drivers. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SEAN SIROTA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230629ej6t00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
