\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Starbucks Union Plans To Strike at 150 Stores Over Pride D\u233\'00\'E9cor Rules\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. Edward Moreno and Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 877 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Walkouts were called at more than 150 stores in the next week after workers said Pride Month decorations had been banned in some, a claim the company denied.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of workers at organized \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores across the nation will stage strikes over the next week, their union said on Friday, after workers in some states said management prohibited them from putting up decorations for Pride Month.\par \par \uc2 The company denied the accusations and issued a statement declaring that it ''has been and will continue to be at the forefront of supporting the LGBTQIA2+ community.''\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United said employees at more than 150 stores would strike over the company's labor practices and its ''hypocritical treatment of LGBTQIA+ workers.''\par \par \uc2 The union represents about 8,000 of the company's workers in more than 300 stores.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers in a number of stores said this month that they had been told that no decorations for the annual L.G.B.T.Q. celebration, such as rainbow flags, were allowed this year, a shift from previous years. In interviews arranged through their union, workers said the given reasons varied.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , which has roughly 9,300 corporate-owned stores in the United States, has said decoration policies are often specific to each store.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  official involved in the response to the union campaign said the company decided last year, after the union campaign began to spread across the country, to be more aggressive in enforcing dress codes and policies on what could be posted in stores. The official, who spoke on the condition of anonymity, attributed the change to concern that many stores would otherwise become inundated with union paraphernalia.\par \par \uc2 But a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokeswoman on Friday called the claim ''false'' and said there had been no change in the company's guidance on displays and decorations in the past nine years.\par \par \uc2 A corporate statement -- over the names of \uc2 Laxman Narasimhan\uc2 , the chief executive, and Sara Trilling, executive vice president and president, North America -- did not address store-by-store practices. But it noted that for Pride Month, the rainbow flag was being flown over the company's Seattle headquarters and in thousands of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores.\par \par \uc2 ''We continue to encourage our store leaders to celebrate with their communities including for U.S. Pride Month in June,'' it said.\par \par \uc2 Casey Moore, a union spokeswoman, derided the corporate statement, saying, ''Instead of apologizing for there being upper management across the country who made the decision to not allow Pride decorations, they've doubled down that it didn't happen.''\par \par \uc2 In addition to its complaints over the Pride decoration issue, the union said it was striking over the company's broader response to the organizing campaign, including widespread retaliation against union supporters. The union said in its statement that workers were ''demanding that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  negotiate a fair contract with union stores and stop their illegal union-busting campaign.''\par \par \uc2 The company has consistently denied accusations of illegality.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers and the union say rules on employee conduct have been enforced more aggressively as a way to intimidate and retaliate against union supporters.\par \par \uc2 ''They're trying to make people feel unwelcome in whatever way possible -- through more strict enforcement of the dress code or anything,'' said Ms. Moore, the union spokeswoman. ''The Pride decorations are another level of that.''\par \par \uc2 In a sweeping ruling in March, a federal administrative law judge found that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had repeatedly violated labor law by ''more strictly enforcing the dress code and personal appearance policy in response to union activity.'' The judge also found that the company had more strictly enforced its attendance policy and its policy on soliciting and distributing notices within stores.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has disputed the findings and is appealing the decision to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Unionized Starbucks workers have staged waves of strikes in the last several months over what they say are the company's delay tactics at the bargaining table and other anti-union tactics like retaliatory firings and store closings. The administrative judge's ruling in March also found that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had illegally dismissed seven Buffalo-area workers last year in response to union activity.\par \par \uc2 In April, the labor board issued a complaint accusing the company of failing to bargain in good faith at more than 100 stores. It was one of dozens of complaints tied to labor law violations that the board has issued since the union first filed petitions seeking votes in three Buffalo-area stores in August 2021.\par \par \uc2 The company has denied the accusations and blames the union for bargaining delays, citing the union's insistence on using video-chat software to broadcast sessions to employees not at the bargaining table.\par \par \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , shortly after stepping down as \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ' chief executive in March, denied allegations of anti-union conduct in testimony before a Senate committee.\par \par \uc2 A rally at a Starbucks in Seattle on Friday. Some workers in the U.S. said they were not allowed to put up rainbow flags this year. Starbucks said policies are up to the store. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MATT MILLS MCKNIGHT/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page B6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230624ej6o0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
