\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Why There's Progress, Finally, on Closing the Black-White Pay Gap\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Leonhardt \par \uc2 892 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the early 2000s, the wage gap between Black and white workers in the U.S. was as large as it had been in 1950.\par \par \uc2 That is a shocking statistic and a sign of the country's deep racial inequality. Over the last five years, however, the story has changed somewhat: The wage gap, though still enormous, has shrunk. ''It's a pretty meaningful reversal,'' \uc2 Elise Gould\uc2 , a senior economist at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , told me.\par \par \uc2 But why the gap has narrowed and what would have to happen for it to narrow more? After all, even with the recent progress, the median Black worker makes 21 percent less than the median white worker.\par \par \uc2 A Strong Economy\par \par \uc2 There appear to be three main causes of the recent trend, and the most significant is the country's tight labor market. The unemployment rate has been falling for most of the last decade and has recently been near its lowest levels since the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 Tight labor markets help almost all workers, and they tend to help disadvantaged workers the most. As Gould put it, ''When employers can't be quite as choosy -- when employers have to look beyond their network -- that can provide more opportunities for historically marginalized groups.''\par \par \uc2 This dynamic helps close the Black-white wage gap because Black workers are overrepresented among low-wage workers.\par \par \uc2 The Hispanic-white wage gap has also declined recently.\par \par \uc2 William Spriggs, a labor economist and \uc2 Howard University\uc2  professor who died unexpectedly this month, often made this point. In one of his last interviews, Spriggs told my colleague Ben Casselman that he was concerned the recent Federal Reserve interest-rate increases would weaken the labor market and undo the recent progress of Black workers.\par \par \uc2 ''You should see from this moment what you are truly risking,'' Spriggs said.\par \par \uc2 Of course, inflation is also a serious economic problem, which is why the Fed has raised rates. But the recent narrowing of racial wage inequality is a reminder that the Fed faces risks both from doing too little to fight inflation and from doing too much. ''Tight labor markets make almost everything else easier,'' said Suzanne Kahn, a historian who works at the Roosevelt Institute, a think tank.\par \par \uc2 The Fight for $15\par \par \uc2 More than a decade ago, a group of fast-food workers in New York City began agitating for a higher minimum wage. They attracted the support of Senator Bernie Sanders, the leaders of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  and other high-profile allies. The movement became known as the Fight for $15.\par \par \uc2 It has not persuaded Congress to lift the federal minimum wage, mostly because of opposition from congressional Republicans. The federal hourly minimum has been $7.25 since 2009, even as inflation has eroded its value. But the Fight for $15 movement has helped change policy in states and cities.\par \par \uc2 A minimum wage well above $7.25 is a broadly popular idea, including among many Republican voters and independents. Ballot initiatives to raise the minimum wage have passed over the last decade in Arizona, Arkansas, Colorado, Florida, Missouri, Nebraska, Nevada and several other states. As a result, the effective national minimum wage -- a weighted average of state minimum wages, adjusted for inflation -- has risen to nearly its highest level in 40 years (before falling a bit lately because of high inflation.)\par \par \uc2 Minimum-wage increases tend to shrink the racial wage gap for the same reason that tight labor markets do: Black workers disproportionately work in low-wage jobs. As a result, one powerful way to reduce racial inequality is to reduce economic inequality.\par \par \uc2 The flip is also true. The racial wage gap widened in the 1980s, 1990s and early 2000s mostly because income inequality was soaring.\par \par \uc2 Black Lives Matter\par \par \uc2 After a Minneapolis police officer murdered George Floyd on May 25, 2020, racial inequity became a focus of intense national attention. Many companies promised to diversify their workforces and leadership ranks, and some took concrete action.\par \par \uc2 At Fortune 500 companies, for example, Black board members occupied less than 9 percent of all board seats in 2020, according to \uc2 Deloitte\uc2 . By last year, the number had risen to 12 percent (compared with 14 percent of the U.S. population). It remains unclear how widespread the changes in corporate America have been; corporate boards obviously make up a tiny share of jobs. But the recent emphasis on diversity has probably played at least a modest role in narrowing racial gaps.\par \par \uc2 There is a larger point here. Yes, a reduction in economic inequality can substantially shrink the Black-white wage gap. But that gap will never approach zero so long as racial inequities remain as large as they are in the U.S. today.\par \par \uc2 The problem is not only that Black workers disproportionately work in low-wage job categories; it's also that Black Americans make less money on average than similar white Americans. According to the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , a typical Black worker last year made 13 percent less than a typical white worker who was the same age and gender, had the same amount of education and lived in the same region. And the racial wealth gap is even larger than the wage gap.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230620ej6j0000a\par }\page {\par\fs2