\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 UPS Workers Authorize Union to Call Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A walkout is possible after the contract for more than 325,000 workers expires this summer. Negotiations began in April but have yet to resolve pay.\par \par \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  workers have authorized their union, the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , to call a strike as soon as Aug. 1, after the current contract expires, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  announced Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  represent more than 325,000 \uc2 UPS\uc2  employees in the United States, where the company has nearly 450,000 employees overall. The union said 97 percent had voted in favor of strike authorization.\par \par \uc2 Many unions hold such votes to create leverage at the bargaining table, but a much smaller percentage end up following through. ''The results do not mean a strike is imminent and do not impact our current business operations in any way,'' \uc2 UPS\uc2  said in a statement, adding that it was ''confident that we will reach an agreement.''\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 UPS\uc2  strike could have significant economic fallout. The company handles about one-quarter of the tens of millions of parcels shipped each day in the United States, according to the Pitney Bowes Parcel Shipping Index. And while \uc2 UPS\uc2 's competition has grown in recent years, rivals would be hard-pressed to replace that lost capacity quickly, leaving some customers in the lurch and others facing higher costs.\par \par \uc2 ''What happens when you try to stuff 25 percent more food into a stomach that's 90 percent full?'' said Alan Amling, a fellow at the \uc2 University of Tennessee\uc2 's Global Supply Chain Institute and a former \uc2 UPS\uc2  executive.\par \par \uc2 The two sides have reached tentative agreements on a number of issues since they began negotiating a national contract in April, most recently on heat safety, including a requirement for air conditioning in new trucks beginning in January and additional fans and venting for existing trucks.\par \par \uc2 But the negotiators have yet to tackle pay increases, which the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  say are overdue amid the company's strong pandemic-era performance. The company's adjusted net income increased by more than 70 percent from 2019 to last year.\par \par \uc2 The union has also focused on revisiting pay disparities for a category of driver who typically works on weekends.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UPS\uc2  chief executive, \uc2 Carol Tom\u233\'00\'E9\uc2 , who started in that position in 2020, said on a recent earnings call that \uc2 UPS\uc2  was aligned with the union on ''several key issues.'' She added that outsiders should not put too much stock in the ''great deal of noise'' that was likely to arise during the negotiation.\par \par \uc2 Looming over the talks is the political standing of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' leader, Sean O'Brien, who during his campaign for the union's presidency in 2021 repeatedly accused his predecessor, \uc2 James P. Hoffa\uc2 , of being overly conciliatory toward employers.\par \par \uc2 Mr. O'Brien complained that Mr. Hoffa had essentially forced a concessionary contract onto \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers in 2018 after union members voted down the deal. He criticized his opponent for the presidency, a Hoffa-aligned candidate, for being unlikely to strike.\par \par \uc2 ''You already conceded that in your 25-year career, you only struck six times, so \uc2 UPS\uc2  knows you're not going to strike,'' Mr. O'Brien said at a candidates' debate.\par \par \uc2 Mr. O'Brien has largely maintained his aggressive stance on \uc2 UPS\uc2  since taking over as president last year. Speaking in October to activists with \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  for a Democratic Union, a reformist group that backed his candidacy, Mr. O'Brien vowed that ''this \uc2 UPS\uc2  agreement is going to be the defining moment in organized labor.''\par \par \uc2 Compensation for \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers is generally higher than pay at the company's competitors. \uc2 UPS\uc2  said that the average full-time delivery driver with four years' experience makes $42 an hour, and that part-time workers who sort packages make $20 an hour on average after 30 days.\par \par \uc2 The groups receive the same benefits package, which includes health care and pension contributions and is worth about $50,000 a year for full-time drivers, the company says.\par \par \uc2 Beyond overall pay levels, the union has said it wants to eliminate a category of driver created under the 2018 contract.\par \par \uc2 The company said the category was intended for hybrid workers who performed jobs like sorting packages on some days while driving on other days, especially Saturdays, to address the growing demand for weekend delivery.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  said these workers never followed the hybrid arrangement and simply drove full time from Tuesday to Saturday, for less pay than other full-time drivers. The company says that the weekend drivers make about 87 percent of the base pay of regular full-time drivers, and that some employees have worked under a hybrid arrangement.\par \par \uc2 In the event of a strike, deliveries to consumers, such as e-commerce orders, would probably be among the first to be disrupted. But experts said the supply chain could suffer, too. Some suppliers would struggle to quickly ship goods like automotive parts to manufacturers, potentially causing production slowdowns.\par \par \uc2 Even a short strike could take a toll on \uc2 UPS\uc2 . Many customers long relied exclusively on the company, but that started to change after the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  last went on strike in 1997, Mr. Amling said. After that strike, which lasted just over two weeks, more customers began to work with multiple carriers. The consequences were masked by gains from the rise of e-commerce and fewer competitors to choose from, but the company may not be so fortunate today.\par \par \uc2 Niraj Chokshi contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Niraj Chokshi contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 The Teamsters represent more than 325,000 UPS employees in the U.S., and a prolonged strike could disrupt e-commerce orders and the supply chain. (PHOTOGRAPH BYB6.A2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230617ej6h0004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
