\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 West Coast Dockworkers Reach a Deal With Ports\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kurtis Lee \par \uc2 901 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After a year of prolonged negotiations that have led to delays and declines in cargo, the two sides agreed to a new contract with help from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 After a year of contract negotiations that resulted in numerous delays and a decline in the movement of cargo at ports along the West Coast, union dockworkers and port operators have reached a tentative deal set to last for six years.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement released late Wednesday, the \uc2 International Longshore and Warehouse Union\uc2  and the \uc2 Pacific Maritime Association\uc2  announced a tentative agreement on a new contract that covers 22,000 workers at 29 ports from San Diego to Seattle, some of the busiest in the world.\par \par \uc2 Details about the agreement, which is expected to be formally ratified by both sides, were not immediately released.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, who stepped in last year to urge a swift resolution, released a statement congratulating the two parties for reaching an agreement ''after a long and sometimes acrimonious negotiation.''\par \par \uc2 ''As I have always said, collective bargaining works,'' Mr. Biden said. ''Above all I congratulate the port workers, who have served heroically through the pandemic and the countless challenges it brought and will finally get the pay, benefits and quality of life they deserve.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden also thanked Julie Su, the acting U.S. labor secretary, for assistance in finalizing the deal. Ms. Su, who in recent years served as head of the \uc2 California Labor and Workforce Development Agency\uc2 , was nominated by Mr. Biden in February to succeed \uc2 Martin J. Walsh\uc2  as labor secretary. For months, her nomination has languished before a closely divided Senate.\par \par \uc2 The outcome on Wednesday somewhat mirrored past negotiations. In 2015, as talks went on for nine months, officials in the Obama administration intervened amid work slowdowns and increased congestion at ports.\par \par \uc2 It is also the second time in six months that the Biden administration has intervened in a labor dispute that could have caused major damage to the U.S. economy. In December, Mr. Biden signed legislation to impose a labor agreement between rail companies and workers.\par \par \uc2 On the horizon is the prospect of another disruption to the nation's freight system. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  contract with \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 , covering 340,000 workers, expires on July 31, and the union will announce the results of a strike authorization vote on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The negotiations between the Longshore and Warehouse Union and the \uc2 Pacific Maritime Association\uc2 , which represents the shipping terminals, have focused on disagreements over wages and the expanding role of automation. (Unionized workers at the ports have average salaries in the low six figures.) Last year, the two sides, who remained quiet on negotiations for much of the year, announced incremental agreements on areas including health care benefits.\par \par \uc2 Workers have staged a series of work slowdowns at the ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach, which in recent months have lost sizable business to ports along the Gulf and East Coasts. Cargo processing at the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 , a key entry point for shipments from Asia, was down roughly 40 percent in February, compared with the year before.\par \par \uc2 For decades, up and down the West Coast, ports have created an ecosystem of jobs that span warehouses, trucking and railroads. In Oakland, for example, more than 84,000 regional jobs rely on the city's port. Roughly 13,000 truck drivers are authorized to pick up cargo at the ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach.\par \par \uc2 Christopher S. Tang, a distinguished professor at the \uc2 University of California, Los Angeles\uc2 , Anderson School of Management, who studies supply chains, said it was unclear whether shipping volumes in the nation's Pacific ports would rebound.\par \par \uc2 ''Many firms have shifted their supply base from China to Southeast Asia and Mexico,'' said Mr. Tang. ''For many Southeast Asian countries, it is cheaper and faster to ship to the East Coast instead of the West Coast.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tang added, ''West Coast ports need to do more marketing to woo shippers, importers and exporters back.''\par \par \uc2 Recently, the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2  wrote to Mr. Biden urging the administration to intervene immediately in the negotiations and appoint an independent mediator to help the two parties reach an agreement.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Shay\uc2 , president of the \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 , said the ongoing delays and disruptions have had a negative impact on retailers and other stakeholders who rely on the West Coast ports for business operations.\par \par \uc2 ''As we enter the all-important peak shipping season for holiday merchandise, retailers need a seamless flow of containers through the ports and to their distribution centers,'' Mr. Shay said.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, \uc2 Gene Seroka\uc2 , head of the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 , said in a statement that the tentative agreement between the I.L.W.U. and the Pacific Maritime ''brings the stability and confidence that customers have been seeking.''\par \par \uc2 Matt Schrap, chief executive of the Harbor Trucking Association, a trade group for transportation companies serving West Coast ports, said his organization is eager for cargo traffic to return to normal soon.\par \par \uc2 ''We need the certainty,'' he said. ''This has been a long, hard process.''\par \par \uc2 The tentative agreement, which still must be ratified, covers 22,000 workers at 29 ports from San Diego to Seattle. (PHOTOGRAPH BY STELLA KALININA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B1, B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230616ej6g0005a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
