\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New York Delivery Workers Are Getting a Bump in Pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stefanos Chen \par \uc2 1,155 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The increase, which requires app-based delivery workers to be paid $17.96 per hour before tips, is the first such minimum wage in the country for an industry that has exploded in popularity.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps more than any other American city, New York relies on a growing army of delivery workers who have braved successive waves of Covid, extreme weather and toxic air as remote work has reshaped the economy. Now, they're getting a raise.\par \par \uc2 Starting July 12, New York City's app-based delivery workers must be paid at least $17.96 an hour, not including tips -- the first such minimum pay-rate in the country for an industry that exploded in popularity during the pandemic. The increase, which will go into effect nearly two years after the City Council passed a set of bills designed to improve conditions for the workers, was announced by Mayor Eric Adams over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Critics say the rule does not go far enough to compensate the workers, who must absorb a range of expenses as independent contractors, including frequent injuries on the job. And industry groups argue that the added costs could throttle opportunities for some workers and pass on costs to consumers and restaurants, which already pay high fees to use the apps.\par \par \uc2 The city's more than 60,000 delivery workers, who courier takeout, groceries and other goods, are paid an average of about $11 an hour, after factoring in tips and expenses, less than New York's $15 minimum wage, according to an analysis by the city. They also cover their own health insurance, business expenses and additional taxes.\par \par \uc2 The new law, first proposed in 2021, will eventually raise the workers' minimum wage to at least $19.96 an hour in 2025, or more, based on inflation.\par \par \uc2 ''This is huge and historic for an entire industry that doesn't have protections,'' said Ligia Guallpa, the executive director of the Worker's Justice Project, a labor advocacy group that pushed for the law. ''It will have a huge economic impact on workers and their families.''\par \par \uc2 Daron Harris, 34, an Uber Eats driver from Far Rockaway, Queens, who was waiting for a job outside of a \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  in Midtown Manhattan on Monday afternoon, was thrilled about the coming pay increase.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel like it is so well-deserved, because some days are good and others are really bad,'' he said, adding that he receives just $2.50 to $3.50 per delivery, not including tips.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Harris, who makes deliveries for about 35 hours a week and also works as a security guard, said he earns about $150 through Uber Eats ''on a good day,'' $23 of which he must spend to rent an electric bike.\par \par \uc2 ''With this new wage, basically you are guaranteed to make a decent amount of money every day,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 But the true benefit to delivery workers could be smaller than advertised, critics said. Brad Lander, the city comptroller, who first sponsored the bill as a City Council member in 2021, said the city had watered down the intent of the law, after months of delays, in the face of lobbying pressure.\par \par \uc2 The actual increased wage would be less than $13 an hour, not close to $20 an hour, as the city suggests, Mr. Lander said, in part because the rule includes a so-called multi-app deduction that considers how workers may be logged into more than one app at a time.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lander also criticized the city's decision to stagger the wage increases through 2025, when the workers already make below minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 The law allows delivery companies to meet the new pay requirements this year through two different models: either by paying a flat hourly rate, which is not common in the industry, or by paying per delivery, at a rate of about 50 cents per minute, not including tips.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the mayor's office said the rate increase would lead to ''significantly higher'' wages for the workers, and that Mr. Lander's wage calculation was incorrect.\par \par \uc2 The multi-app adjustment is appropriate, the spokesman said, based on a study of how workers use the apps, and the Department of Consumer and Worker Protection will review and possibly revise the rule next year. The city is staggering the pay increases over a period of two years in order to give delivery app companies time to adjust to the new rates, he said.\par \par \uc2 Industry groups opposed the law. Kristin Sharp, the chief executive of Flex, a trade association that represents delivery apps including \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , Uber Eats and \uc2 Instacart\uc2 , said the added costs to companies could result in higher fees for consumers, and the reduction or possible elimination of tips for workers.\par \par \uc2 Some companies might also limit access to the app to some lower frequency workers, she said, depending on which model of payment the companies pursue.\par \par \uc2 But similarly bleak predictions did not come true when the for-hire vehicle industry, a close analog for gig workers, was forced to raise wages in New York in 2019, said James Parrott, the director of economic and fiscal policy at the Center for New York City Affairs at the New School.\par \par \uc2 A 2020 study on the effects of that pay raise found that driver pay increased 9 percent and passenger fares rose about 5.9 percent, in line with fare increases seen in Chicago, where a minimum pay standard was not established. Customer wait times also declined, the study found.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Parrott, who consulted with the city on delivery worker pay, said he did not expect the wage increases to fundamentally change the food-delivery landscape in New York, and he predicted that demand would continue to grow.\par \par \uc2 The number of delivery workers in the city has more than doubled since before the pandemic, he said, from roughly 25,000 to 30,000 workers in 2019 to more than 60,000 today.\par \par \uc2 And the delivery companies have significant margins to help offset the wage increases, he said. Last year, workers made about $4.32 per delivery, not including tips, while delivery companies made a gross profit of $4.19 on average, according to a city report.\par \par \uc2 ''It shouldn't be tolerated that business can operate only if they can exploit their workers,'' he said. ''If this gets us away from a worker-exploitive business model, this seems like a reasonable trade off.''\par \par \uc2 Erin Nolan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Erin Nolan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 The 60,000 app-based couriers who ferry takeout, groceries and other goods to New York City residents must be paid at least $17.96 an hour, not including tips, as of mid-July and at least $19.96 an hour in 2025. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KARSTEN MORAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230613ej6d0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
