\par\par \uc2 JAMELLE BOUIE\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The Court Is Hostile to U.S. Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamelle Bouie \par \uc2 998 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It is difficult to overstate the hostility of the Roberts court to organized labor and the rights of American workers.\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , who became chief justice in 2005, the court has made it harder for workers to bring suit against employers collectively, limited the power of workers to hold employers responsible for discrimination on the job, ended the ability of public sector unions to require dues from nonmembers who benefit from collective bargaining and struck down a California law that allowed unions to recruit workers on the property of agricultural employers.\par \par \uc2 In pretty much any given conflict between an employer and a group of workers, you can count on Roberts and his Republican allies on the court to side with the employer.\par \par \uc2 We saw this dynamic at work last week when the court issued its decision in \uc2 Glacier Northwest\uc2  v. International Brotherhood of Teamsters Local Union No. 174. The case involves a struggle in Washington State between workers represented by the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and their employer, a concrete manufacturer.\par \par \uc2 In its lawsuit, Glacier alleged that its workers timed a 2017 strike so that it would begin after some of the company's mixing trucks were already filled with wet concrete, a perishable material. Glacier's non-unionized workers were able to remove the concrete before the trucks were significantly damaged, but the company sued the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  in state court anyway for damages relating to lost revenue from the wrecked concrete.\par \par \uc2 The union countered, citing the right to strike. It also noted that the damaged concrete was essentially spoilage of a product, for which unions have not generally been held liable. The Washington State Supreme Court dismissed the suit on the grounds that the dispute was ''pre-empted by the National Labor Relations Act.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court took Glacier's appeal. And in an opinion joined by Roberts and Justices \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  held that unions are liable for damages during strikes under federal labor law when they take ''affirmative steps to endanger'' the employer's property rather than ''reasonable precautions to mitigate that risk.'' She also sent the case back to the Washington State court for further litigation.\par \par \uc2 In a separate concurrence joined by \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said the Supreme Court should reconsider its 1959 decision in San Diego Building Trades Council v. Garmon, which held that state courts are barred from handling claims concerning conduct that is ''arguably'' covered by the \uc2 National Labor Relations\uc2  Act. Under Garmon, employers must first receive a favorable ruling from the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  if they want to sue a union for striking in state court. Tossing Garmon would bring labor law much closer to its pre-N.L.R.A. status quo, when conservative judges treated union actions as little more than criminal conspiracies to harm employers. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  also filed a concurrence in support of the majority.\par \par \uc2 The divide among the liberal justices was especially striking. The sole dissent came from Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , who argued that the ruling would ''erode the right to strike'' and undermine the oversight of workplace law by the N.L.R.B. ''Workers are not indentured servants, bound to continue laboring until any planned work stoppage would be as painless as possible for their master,'' she wrote. ''They are employees whose collective and peaceful decision to withhold their labor is protected by the N.L.R.A. even if economic injury results.''\par \par \uc2 It is possible that Justices Kagan and Sotomayor joined Barrett's opinion in a strategic move meant to foreclose a more expansive decision from Thomas, Gorsuch and Alito. If so, it may ultimately prove a short reprieve in the face of a conservative majority that is eager to undermine a set of interests (labor's interests) and a set of rights (workers' rights) that it does not respect.\par \par \uc2 One point that must be emphasized is how, with its war on workers, the Roberts court is only acting in the Supreme Court's historical capacity as an agent of capital. At times, the court has taken an expansive view of the civil and political rights of the American people. But it has rarely been a friend to the right of workers to organize and act in their own interests.\par \par \uc2 In the decade before the passage of the National Labor Relations Act, for example, the Supreme Court under William Howard Taft issued rulings constraining the ability of unions to act and organize, subjecting union actions to antitrust law and upholding restrictions on speech that targeted unions and other pro-labor organizations.\par \par \uc2 In other words, the Supreme Court is first and foremost the leading defender of property within our political order. And how could it be otherwise? The Constitution itself was written, in part, to protect the rights of property in the face of democracy and the spirit of egalitarianism. Even a more liberal Supreme Court than the one we have now would eventually find itself acting against labor, for the simple reason that the American political system was not built with the interests of workers in mind.\par \par \uc2 This means, as our actual court has again made clear, that the struggle for the emancipation of labor does not, as Samuel Gompers once wrote, take place in an ''ideal world.'' Instead, ''we are in the bitter struggles of an unjust society.'' If labor is ever going to get what it needs, it probably won't be with the helping hand of a judge or a justice.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page SR3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230611ej6b00031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
