\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Actors Vote to Authorize Strike, While Writers Continue Picketing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Sperling \par \uc2 511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 The union that represents more than 160,000 film and television actors voted on Monday night to authorize a strike, two days before it is to begin negotiations on a new labor deal with the Hollywood studios. The result from members of the \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  union, with 98 percent authorizing a strike, was expected, and it came during the sixth week of a strike by Hollywood writers and just a day after the \uc2 Directors Guild of America\uc2  tentatively agreed to a new contract.\par \par \uc2 ''Together we lock elbows, and in unity we build a new contract that honors our contributions in this remarkable industry, reflects the new digital and streaming business model and brings ALL our concerns for protections and benefits into the now!'' Fran Drescher, the president of the actors' union, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 About 65,000 members cast ballots, or 48 percent of eligible voters. The actors' current agreement with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , which bargains on behalf of the studios, expires on June 30.\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters: The actors have the same worries as the writers.\par \par \uc2 Many of the actors' concerns echo what the Writers Guild of America is fighting for: higher wages; increased residual payments for their work, specifically for content on streaming services; and protections against using actors' likenesses without permission as part of the enhanced abilities of artificial intelligence. According to the writers, the studios offered little more than ''annual meetings to discuss'' artificial intelligence, and they refused to bargain over limits on the technology.\par \par \uc2 The Directors Guild, in contrast, said on Sunday that it had reached a ''groundbreaking agreement confirming that A.I. is not a person and that generative A.I. cannot replace the duties performed by members.'' Details about what that meant were not revealed.\par \par \uc2 Background: It has been a long time since the last actors' strike.\par \par \uc2 The last time the actors went on strike was in 2000, in a dispute over commercial pay. The strike lasted close to six months.\par \par \uc2 What's Next: Negotiations begin on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 With negotiations expected to begin on Wednesday, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  is bullish about what this strike authorization means. ''We're obviously coming in from a position of strength, but we're not looking to strike,'' said Duncan Crabtree-Ireland, the union's chief negotiator. ''We're here to make a deal.'' He added: ''But we're also not going to accept anything less than what our members deserve. If a strike is necessary to achieve that, we're prepared.''\par \par \uc2 The Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers said in a statement that ''we are approaching these negotiations with the goal of achieving a new agreement that is beneficial to \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members and the industry overall.''\par \par \uc2 Members of SAG-AFTRA supported the striking Writers Guild of America at a rally last month outside Warner Bros. Studios in Burbank, Calif. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CHRIS PIZZELLO/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230607ej6700037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
