\par\par \uc2 THE BIG NUMBER\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 49%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg \par \uc2 330 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Average office occupancy rate, compared with prepandemic levels, across major U.S. cities.\par \par \uc2 Cubicles sit empty, printers gather dust, and office pantries wait for the one day a week when their workers might actually come in, craving the company of their teammates.\par \par \uc2 More than three years since Covid arrived and upended the workplace, many forms of social life have bounced back to nearly their prepandemic levels. Air travel at the end of 2022 was almost back to where it was in 2019. But many offices remain stubbornly quiet, even as executives roll out perks to entice employees to return, offering elaborate free lunches and company-branded swag.\par \par \uc2 Some workers say they prepared too many times for return-to-office dates that never came to pass. And when they did, many employees were already attached to the habits they had formed while working from home, like homemade lunches and zero-minute commutes. They don't want to return to their offices only to sit on video calls with colleagues who are still working from home. Some workers, like those at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , are outright protesting return-to-office rules.\par \par \uc2 Business leaders have often found that the best way to fill the office is to remind employees of its social promise. It turns out people enjoy spending time with co-workers more than they remember -- which could mean a more coordinated approach to R.T.O., like bringing workers back on Tuesdays and Wednesdays (the most popular office days, according to data from the security firm \uc2 Kastle\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 ''They actually forgot how great it was to actually interact with human beings,'' said Sasan Godarzi, the head of \uc2 Intuit\uc2 , which has allowed teams and managers to set return-to-office expectations.\par \par \uc2 But even if the biggest R.T.O. perk is the people, that still requires figuring out how to get all of them to come back.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page BU2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230604ej640005a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
