\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Strong Summer Job Market Awaits Teens\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ann Carrns \par \uc2 1,209 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amusement parks, restaurants, pools and other entertainment spots will need workers. Here's what to know about finding a job.\par \par \uc2 Teenagers seeking work will probably find ample jobs with good pay available this summer, economists say.\par \par \uc2 The robust labor market this year, along with shortages of workers in the summer jobs that teenagers usually fill, like in hospitality and leisure, suggests good prospects, said Paul Harrington, a labor economist at \uc2 Rhode Island College\uc2  who is part of a team that produces an annual summer job forecast for teenagers.\par \par \uc2 Their analysis predicts that the share of 16- to 19-year-olds working this season will rise to 33.6 percent, from 32.7 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 The outlook is strong despite worries about a potential economic slowdown. The labor market overall has proved resilient, with 339,000 jobs added in May even as the unemployment rate ticked up to 3.7 percent, from 3.4 percent in April.\par \par \uc2 ''Obviously, there are storm clouds over the summer outlook,'' said \uc2 Nick Bunker\uc2 , the director of North American economic research at the job search website Indeed. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been raising interest rates to cool inflation, stirring concerns about a possible recession.\par \par \uc2 Even so, Mr. Bunker said demand to fill positions this summer was strong. Alicia Sasser Modestino, an associate professor of public policy and urban affairs and economics at \uc2 Northeastern University\uc2  in Boston, agreed, though ''it may not be quite as gangbusters as last summer,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic kept job opportunities tight in 2020, but summer employment rebounded the next year and remained strong last summer.\par \par \uc2 Teen summer wages have risen in recent years, even after accounting for inflation, Mr. Harrington said. Last summer, the median hourly pay for teenagers rose to $14, from $11.50 in 2019. In some parts of the country, pay for certain jobs, lifeguards in particular, has spiked. In New York City, a shortage of lifeguards has pushed wages above $20 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Restaurants expect to add 502,000 seasonal jobs this summer, the strongest hiring picture since 2017, according to the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2 . (The group did not include numbers for the past three summers, saying the pandemic years were ''not typical'' hiring seasons.)\par \par \uc2 In May, the unemployment rate for teenagers rose to 10.3 percent, up from 9.2 percent in April but little changed from 10.5 percent a year ago, according to seasonally adjusted data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  bureau.\par \par \uc2 The outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2  expects that more than one million jobs will be created for teenagers this summer, slightly fewer than last year. However, young people have already been working at higher rates than they had in years, said Andy Challenger, the company's senior vice president. In March, about 5.48 million workers ages 16 to 19 were employed, the firm noted, the highest total since 2007, when 5.61 million teenagers were employed (based on data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  that was not seasonally adjusted).\par \par \uc2 While amusement parks, pools, restaurants and other entertainment spots will certainly need summer workers, Mr. Challenger said, the question is whether teenagers will take the jobs. ''So many teens who want to work are already working,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Still, those seeking summer work shouldn't wait to apply, Mr. Bunker, from Indeed, said. ''Don't wait until late June.''\par \par \uc2 Here are some questions and answers about teenagers and summer jobs:\par \par \uc2 I'm swamped with studies and activities during the school year. Why should I work in the summer?\par \par \uc2 Many students must work in the summer to support themselves and their families or save for college. But even if it's just for extra spending money, the benefits of a summer job go beyond earning cash, said Amy Carney, a mother of six and the author of ''Parent on Purpose,'' a book about raising children.\par \par \uc2 Teenagers, she said, ''become stronger through the uncomfortable and inconvenient experiences that come with working at a local restaurant, grocery store or other service industry jobs.''\par \par \uc2 Isaac Hertenstein, 17 and a rising senior in Greencastle, Ind., started a nonprofit that recruits student volunteers to teach money skills to younger students. He agreed about the importance of summer jobs.\par \par \uc2 ''It's the ultimate financial literacy lesson for teenagers,'' he said, adding that summer jobs reinforce basics like showing up on time and the value of money. ''It's a big difference,'' he said, if your parents give you $10 for lunch with friends, ''or if it's money you actually worked for.'' He said he hoped to work as a landscaper to save for college and will gain experience as a research assistant in consumer science at a university.\par \par \uc2 Tim Ranzetta, a founder of Next Gen Personal Finance, a nonprofit that makes lessons about money management for schools, said he worked summers as a golf caddie, which helped pay for college and allowed him to spend long hours with golfers who shared insights into the business world. He compared summer work to a financial ''boot camp,'' introducing skills like creating job applications, navigating tax forms, opening a bank account or arranging for direct deposits of paychecks.\par \par \uc2 How can I improve my odds of finding summer work?\par \par \uc2 Apply to multiple jobs, Ms. Modestino, the professor at \uc2 Northeastern University\uc2 , said. Young people can be ''wildly optimistic'' about their hiring prospects, she said, and might think that just because they submitted one application, they will be offered a position. But more applications can yield multiple offers, potentially giving you options for higher pay or flexible schedules.\par \par \uc2 Ask people you know if they are aware of openings, and don't dismiss jobs that might lack an obvious link to your career interests. Aadi Gujral, 17, a rising high school senior in Danville, Calif., who created a money skills app for young people, said he was interested in working in finance and had done projects for his entrepreneur father. But he also said he had worked picking blueberries on his uncle's farm.\par \par \uc2 ''It exposes you to the real world,'' he said. ''And when you are earning your own money, you quickly realize the importance of every dollar.''\par \par \uc2 Can teenagers put summer earnings in a retirement account?\par \par \uc2 Yes. Summer employment can introduce teenagers to the benefit of savings, not only for short-term purchases but also for long-term security, said John Lanza, the author of ''The Art of Allowance,'' a book about teaching children to be financially literate. ''It opens up conversations.''\par \par \uc2 Teenagers with earned income can contribute to a special retirement account known as a Roth individual retirement account. Setting aside even a small amount of earnings can start them on a path toward long-term saving, Mr. Lanza said, and parents may consider matching contributions as an incentive. Unlike money put into a traditional I.R.A., Roth I.R.A. contributions aren't tax-deductible -- but most teenagers don't make enough to pay much income tax, so deductions are less helpful to them. Money invested in a Roth grows tax-free, and can be withdrawn tax-free as long as certain rules are followed.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230603ej630003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
