\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Rising Job Openings Defy Cooling Trend\b0\par\par\uc2 By Talmon Joseph Smith \par \uc2 666 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After three consecutive months of declines, job openings jumped in April, reaching 10.1 million, the Labor Department reported on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The surge signals that job opportunities are withstanding the economic pressures that have led many to believe that the American economy may soon enter a recession.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the report -- known as JOLTS, or the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey -- showed that the labor market was far less feverish than it was a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The quits rate -- viewed as an indicator of how confident workers are in leaving a job and finding employment elsewhere -- was 3 percent, seasonally adjusted, in April 2022. Since then, it has retreated to 2.4 percent, just above its prepandemic peak. And the hiring rate was unchanged from March, which was the lowest since December 2020.\par \par \uc2 Layoffs, however, decreased again, showing that employers are hesitant to let go of employees brought on board during this recovery.\par \par \uc2 The data complicates the interest-rate outlook.\par \par \uc2 The jump in openings may put pressure on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to take interest rates even higher.\par \par \uc2 The statistical relationship between high job vacancies, as calculated by the government, and low unemployment has been frequently cited by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  chair, \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , as a key sign of the labor market's being ''unsustainably hot'' and ''clearly out of balance, with demand for workers substantially exceeding the supply of available workers.''\par \par \uc2 But even as some economists remain unsatisfied with the progress on subduing prices, others worry that reliance on job openings as a core measure of labor market balance may lead the Fed to keep the cost of borrowing for businesses and households too high for too long, prompting a harsher downturn than necessary.\par \par \uc2 ''The quits rate is nearly back to prepandemic levels, the hires rate has already reverted to prepandemic pace,'' Skanda Amarnath, the executive director of Employ America, a nonprofit that supports tight labor markets, wrote in a note. ''JOLTS data should not drastically color this broader assessment of labor market tightness but will matter at the margins for the Fed's own perception of labor market heat.''\par \par \uc2 Some question how much weight to give the report.\par \par \uc2 After peaking at a record of around 12 million in March 2022, job openings as measured by the government have fallen overall. For the past year, a mix of strong hiring for positions that were already listed and a decline in business sentiment has led to a pullback in newly created listings. But the April uptick is at least a pause in recent trends.\par \par \uc2 Some economists think the JOLTS report should be taken with a grain of salt. \uc2 Gregory Daco\uc2 , the chief economist at \uc2 EY\uc2 -Parthenon, said the bump in listings could reflect summer hiring in the rebounding service sector, though he added, ''I'd want to see June before assuming that summer hiring is stronger than last year.''\par \par \uc2 The report is based on a survey of about 21,000 nonfarm business and government establishments. The economic research team at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  has made the case that since the response rate to the JOLTS report has fallen sharply since the start of the pandemic, ''these findings argue for currently treating JOLTS less like the 'true' level of job openings.''\par \par \uc2 The May jobs report will be the next gauge.\par \par \uc2 The May employment report, to be released by the Labor Department on Friday, will fill out the labor market picture before Fed policymakers meet on June 13 and 14.\par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  expect the data to show the addition of 195,000 jobs on a seasonally adjusted basis, down from 253,000 in the initial report for April. Unemployment, which was 3.4 percent in April -- matching the lowest level since 1969 -- is expected to rise to 3.5 percent, and the month-over-month increase in wages is expected to ease.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230601ej6100049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
