\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Tentative Deal Ends Strike By Queens Hospital Doctors\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph Goldstein \par \uc2 490 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The doctors walked off the job earlier this week, in the first strike by New York City hospital doctors in more than 30 years. Their union reached a deal with Mount Sinai, which employs them, on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Resident doctors ended a three-day strike at Elmhurst Hospital Center in Queens after reaching a tentative deal on Wednesday that they say brings them closer to earning as much as their counterparts in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 The strike was the first by hospital doctors in New York City in more than 30 years, and it drew widespread attention in part because of where it occurred. Elmhurst was one of the first hospitals in the United States to be overwhelmed by Covid-19. Descriptions by Elmhurst doctors of desperate and dying patients in March 2020 offered a warning to the rest of the country of what was coming.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has led to a rising tide of activism among doctors. The strike participants, more than 150 in all, were all resident physicians, as newly minted doctors undergoing training in hospitals are called. Their demands included higher pay and stronger guarantees for hazard pay in the event of a future pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Tanathun Kajornsakchai, one of the residents' leaders, said the tentative deal put him and his colleagues ''much closer'' to achieving parity with some of their colleagues in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 ''We got more than we would have gotten otherwise,'' Dr. Kajornsakchai said, adding, ''It's a big fight that this small group of residents has taken on.''\par \par \uc2 Elmhurst is one of the city's 11 publicly run hospitals, which treat many of the city's undocumented immigrants, its working poor and its indigent patients. However, resident physicians working at Elmhurst are not employed by the public hospital system, but by the \uc2 Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai\uc2  in Manhattan. As a result, the city's public hospital system, N.Y.C. Health and Hospitals, was mainly a bystander in the labor dispute.\par \par \uc2 The residents noted that their salaries were lower than what Mount Sinai pays residents at its highly ranked hospital on East 98th Street, opposite Central Park. The salary difference is about $7,000 for first-year residents, they said. Some of the striking doctors said they believed that the pay disparity was related to the fact that many of the resident physicians in the city's public hospitals are foreign nationals who are here on visas.\par \par \uc2 ''The proposed agreement is fair, responsible, and puts patients and residents' educational training first,'' Lucia Lee, a Mount Sinai spokeswoman, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The union representing the striking doctors, the Committee of Interns and Residents, said the doctors would be back at work Thursday morning. The strike, which began on Monday, involved only resident physicians from the departments of internal medicine, pediatrics and psychiatry.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230525ej5p00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
