\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Resident Doctors Go on Strike at Elmhurst in Queens\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph Goldstein \par \uc2 925 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The strike, the first by doctors in New York City since 1990, shows how the pandemic may be leading to rising activism among young doctors.\par \par \uc2 More than 150 trainee doctors went on strike Monday at Elmhurst Hospital Center in Queens, the first physician strike at a hospital in New York City in more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 Chief among their grievances is the fact that they are generally paid less working at a public hospital in Queens, where they care for poor patients, than their counterparts are paid at wealthier Manhattan institutions.\par \par \uc2 Though the strike is relatively small and not expected to result in major disruptions to care, it is heavy on symbolism. Some three years ago, Elmhurst was among the first hospitals in the United States to be overwhelmed by Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Descriptions of panicked, gasping patients, chest compressions and refrigerated morgue trucks -- scenes one Elmhurst doctor described as ''apocalyptic'' -- served as a warning for the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 Now the striking young doctors say the experience of the pandemic has encouraged activism and organizing -- and a growing willingness to challenge the relatively low salaries that resident physicians, as doctors in training are called, receive for working long and grueling hours.\par \par \uc2 Residents at the city's public hospitals have often been reluctant to rock the boat in the past. Many were born and educated abroad and are in the United States on a visa.\par \par \uc2 ''As international residents, we're always so thankful -- we feel very lucky to be here,'' said Dr. Sarah Hafuth, a leader among the resident physicians, who comes from Canada.\par \par \uc2 She added: ''The pandemic was an eye-opener. Physicians really started to question our worth and asking, 'Are we getting the support we need, given the situation we're in?'''\par \par \uc2 At Elmhurst, residents had more difficulty obtaining hazard pay early in the pandemic than those at some Manhattan hospitals, angering many. Hazard pay remains one of the issues driving the strike, one union delegate, a fourth-year psychiatry resident, Dr. Tanathun Kajornsakchai, said.\par \par \uc2 ''It's very empowering for us all to be fighting for the same cause, fighting for fairness, and fighting for parity,'' Dr. Kajornsakchai said in a phone interview Monday from the picket line.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Kajornsakchai was already working at Elmhurst in 2020, though most of his fellow strikers on the picket line were still in medical school back then.\par \par \uc2 One of the signs on the picket line referenced the 7 p.m. nightly cheer -- a brief tradition when New Yorkers opened their windows to clap and bang pots and pans to express thanks to front-line workers.\par \par \uc2 ''Elmhurst was Covid epicenter,'' the sign read. ''Now we're 7 p.m. clapping back.''\par \par \uc2 Physician strikes are a rarity in the United States. The last one in New York City, according to the Committee of Interns and Residents, the union organizing this week's strike, was in 1990 when doctors at a Bronx hospital went on strike for nine days. They won a pay raise and stricter enforcement of rules against working more than 12 hours in a row.\par \par \uc2 Though the striking doctors work at Elmhurst, they are employed by the \uc2 Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai\uc2  in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Doctors at many of New York's 11 public hospitals are affiliated with major Manhattan hospitals and medical schools, the result of longstanding contracts between the city's public hospital system and the city's leading medical institutions.\par \par \uc2 That means that the public hospital system, NYC Health + Hospitals, is largely a bystander in the negotiations. Both the system and Mount Sinai said they had taken steps to minimize interruptions to patient care.\par \par \uc2 Dr. I. Michael Leitman, the dean of graduate medical education at Mount Sinai, said that some Mount Sinai residents had signed up to work Elmhurst shifts during the strike.\par \par \uc2 Among the Elmhurst residents' complaints is that their counterparts working at \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 's main hospital, on East 98th Street, make considerably more.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hafuth said that first-year Elmhurst residents made about $68,000 a year, while residents working at \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 's main campus made $75,000.\par \par \uc2 ''Our patient load is all the same,'' she said. ''We see the same medical pathologies, the same complexity. So we're at a point where we're quite frustrated about why \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  is willing to pay the residents on the Upper East Side more than us.''\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Mount Sinai, Lucia Lee, said in a statement that the health system was ''committed to working toward an equitable and reasonable resolution that is in the best interest for both our residents at Elmhurst as well as for the Mount Sinai Health System.''\par \par \uc2 The strike began at 7 a.m. Monday, drawing resident physicians from the departments of internal medicine, pediatrics and psychiatry. Organizers have called for the strike to last five days and they estimated that more than 150 resident physicians are participating.\par \par \uc2 It comes after more than 10 months of contract negotiations between Mount Sinai and the Committee of Interns and Residents, the union that represents the resident doctors.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lee said that \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 's most recent offer would have paid Elmhurst residents as much as their Mount Sinai counterparts. But Elmhurst residents rejected that characterization, saying the offer would not have put them on the same level. The offer provided for raises between 5 and 7 percent a year, for three years.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230523ej5n0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
