\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Minnesota Senate Passes Bill to Guarantee \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  Drivers Minimum Wage\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kellen Browning \par \uc2 760 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and \uc2 Uber\uc2  have opposed the legislation, threatening to reduce operations or leave the state if it is enacted.\par \par \uc2 The Minnesota Senate passed a bill on Sunday that would guarantee drivers for \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  a minimum wage and other benefits, sending the measure to Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The narrow passage, a 35-32 vote after an earlier 69 to 61 approval from the state's House of Representatives, capped a dramatic week of political maneuvering so the bill would clear the legislature before the session ends on Monday. Drivers for \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  are known as gig workers because they are treated as independent contractors, meaning they are responsible for their own expenses and are not guaranteed a minimum wage, health care or other benefits.\par \par \uc2 If the legislation is signed by the governor, it will require \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  to pay their drivers at least $1.45 per mile they drive a passenger -- or $1.34 per mile outside the Minneapolis-St. Paul region -- as well as $0.34 per minute. It also establishes an appeals process through which drivers can request a review if they feel they have been improperly deactivated from the platforms, and requires additional transparency around how drivers' earnings are calculated.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walz has called the bill ''an important piece of legislation,'' but has also said that more conversations need to happen before he commits to signing it.\par \par \uc2 The bill is a rare win for labor advocates in what has become a protracted, multistate battle over the rights of gig drivers and their status in the economy. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have long argued that their drivers are independent contractors rather than employees. They say that drivers prefer being contractors because it allows them the flexibility to choose when they work, and many drivers work only part-time.\par \par \uc2 But labor advocates contend that drivers are exploited by the companies and are being misclassified as independent even though the ride-hailing services exert significant control over their work.\par \par \uc2 The federal government has largely avoided weighing in on the debate, and the \uc2 U.S. Department of Labor\uc2  has not sued or targeted \uc2 Uber\uc2  or \uc2 Lyft\uc2  for misclassifying workers. Instead, the issue has played out in state courts and legislatures and on ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 New York City and Seattle have passed laws guaranteeing minimum wages for gig drivers, while the companies have prevailed in getting their preferred rules on the books in California and the rest of Washington state. Both states enacted laws that guarantee drivers some benefits, like a minimum wage, but also preclude them from becoming employees. A similar, company-backed effort was thrown out by judges in Massachusetts last year.\par \par \uc2 Senator Omar Fateh, one of the bill's authors, cheered its passage. ''These workers deserve a livable wage to provide for themselves and their families.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fateh and gig drivers from the Minnesota \uc2 Uber\uc2 /Lyft Drivers Association, a group supporting the bill, celebrated outside the legislative chamber on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  said it was disappointed by the bill's passage. ''For months, we have begged legislators to work with us on a compromise that raises rates for drivers without hurting riders, and for months our pleas were ignored,'' Freddi Goldstein, a spokeswoman said, adding, ''We hope Governor Walz will reject this bill.''\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have argued that the bill raises wages too high, and that the deactivation appeals process would limit their ability to bar drivers who have been accused of misconduct.\par \par \uc2 The companies say the extra costs would be passed on to riders, forcing them to pay more, and they have instead proposed a guarantee of $1.17 per mile, as well as $0.34 per minute. \uc2 Uber\uc2  has said it could reduce service in Minnesota -- a threat it has made in the past in other states. \uc2 Lyft\uc2  made a similar threat in a letter to the governor, and said on Sunday, ''We ask that Governor Walz veto the bill and create a task force to properly study the best way to protect drivers while still safeguarding the affordability of the service.''\par \par \uc2 ''If this bill were to pass, we would unfortunately have no choice but to greatly reduce service throughout the state, and possibly shut down operations entirely,'' \uc2 Uber\uc2  said in a message to its Minnesota customers.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  warned its customers that their fares could more than double if the bill is enacted, turning ''ride share into an expensive luxury.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230522ej5m0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
