\par\par \uc2 Movies, Performing Arts/Weekend Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Crater\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brandon Yu \par \uc2 380 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 This \uc2 Disney\uc2  film is surprisingly nimble at incorporating an emotional core into its sci-fi adventure.\par \par \uc2 You wouldn't necessarily expect a lightly dystopian undertone concerning the oppressive state of labor in a family-friendly science-fiction \uc2 Disney\uc2  film (released during the writers' strike, no less), but ''Crater'' manages just that while maintaining the lighthearted fun of a children's adventure.\par \par \uc2 The film, directed by Kyle Patrick Alvarez, takes place on a lunar mining colony, where miners agree to contracts with the promise that they and their families will earn a ticket to Omega, a distant, habitable planet. Legal loopholes, though, ensure that most don't actually live to see that day arrive.\par \par \uc2 [Video: Watch on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .]\par \par \uc2 Yet, via a rule that allows descendants of deceased miners to automatically go to Omega, the film's young protagonist, Caleb (Isaiah Russell-Bailey, and Hero Hunter in flashbacks), is scheduled to leave the colony after his father (Scott Mescudi, a.k.a. Kid Cudi) dies -- only, he doesn't want to leave his friends behind. Hoping to make the most of their limited time together, Caleb and his friends, with the help of a new girl from Earth (McKenna Grace), steal a lunar rover and embark on a road trip in search of a mysterious crater that Caleb's father told him to find as a kind of dying wish.\par \par \uc2 It's refreshing to see \uc2 Disney\uc2  invest a decent budget into an original sci-fi world for a live-action film (it's also a movie that undoubtedly would have flailed at the box office, but may and should find an audience on streaming), and Alvarez makes good use of it. And while it might not have the indelible charm of other children's classics, ''Crater'' does well not straining itself trying to please audiences beyond the family crowd. Most of all, the film is surprisingly nimble at incorporating an emotional core that makes its story more interesting than the adventure itself.\par \par \uc2 Crater\par \par \uc2 Rated PG. Running time: 1 hour 45 minutes. Watch on \uc2 Disney\uc2 +.\par \par \uc2 ''Crater'' is a Disney film that incorporates an emotional core into a sci-fi adventure. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DISNEY+) This article appeared in print on page C8. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230512ej5c00059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
