\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 New \uc2 Lyft\uc2  C.E.O. Lays Out Vision and Tells Workers to Return to the Office\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kellen Browning \par \uc2 939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A day after nearly 1,100 people were laid off, the struggling company told its remaining workers to prepare for a series of changes.\par \par \uc2 Since the pandemic began, \uc2 Lyft\uc2  employees have been able to work remotely, logging into videoconferences from their homes and dispersing across the country like many other tech workers. Last year, the company made that policy official, telling staff that work would be ''fully flexible'' and subleasing floors of its offices in San Francisco and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 No longer. On Friday, David Risher, the company's new chief executive, told employees in an all-hands meeting that they would be required to come back into the office at least three days a week, starting this fall. It was one of the first major changes he has made at the struggling ride-hailing company since starting this month, and it came just a day after he laid off 26 percent of \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's work force.\par \par \uc2 ''Things just move faster when you're face to face,'' Mr. Risher said in an interview. Remote work in the tech industry, he said, had come at a cost, leading to isolation and eroding culture. ''There's a real feeling of satisfaction that comes from working together at a whiteboard on a problem.''\par \par \uc2 The decision, combined with the layoffs and other changes, signals the beginning of a new chapter at \uc2 Lyft\uc2 . It could also be an indication that some tech companies -- particularly firms that are struggling -- may be changing their minds on flexibility about where employees work. Nudges toward working in the office could soon turn into demands, as they have at companies like \uc2 Disney\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bob Sutton, an organizational psychologist and a professor at Stanford, said that although in-person collaboration could help with creativity and some other parts of work, companies pushing for a return to office might be doing so to have greater oversight of their workers.\par \par \uc2 ''When top executives feel financial stress, the classic 'threat-rigidity' effect kicks in, and beyond possible benefits for communication, collaboration and creativity, they feel compelled to increase their own illusion of control,'' Mr. Sutton said.\par \par \uc2 After lagging behind its rival, \uc2 Uber\uc2 , in the race to emerge from the pandemic doldrums, \uc2 Lyft\uc2  posted worrisome financial results in February. Its founders, \uc2 Logan Green\uc2  and \uc2 John Zimmer\uc2 , said the next month that they would step down.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Risher, a veteran of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  who also served on \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's board of directors, has laid out a plan to streamline the business, cut costs and focus on improving the quality and lowering the price of \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's core product: rides for customers.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  employees have complained that divisions outside the ride-hailing business, like units that offer rental cars to its gig drivers and rent bikes and scooters to consumers, seemed to be disproportionately affected by the layoffs. Mr. Risher said the cuts were across the board.\par \par \uc2 He said the cost savings from the layoffs would go toward lower prices for riders and higher earnings for drivers.\par \par \uc2 The next phase of his plan, he said, is to remind riders that \uc2 Lyft\uc2  is a viable alternative to \uc2 Uber\uc2 . In the summer, Mr. Risher said, he will gradually introduce products to increase interest in the platform. That may include teaming up with companies to offer \uc2 Lyft\uc2  rides to their employees who are commuting to offices, he said.\par \par \uc2 The next steps for the company will be difficult. Many \uc2 Lyft\uc2  employees have gotten used to working from home, and some were already bristling at the possibility of returning to the office. \uc2 Lyft\uc2  continues to trail \uc2 Uber\uc2 , which has a global ride-hailing business and also offers food delivery.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's stock price is trading at $10 a share, down from $78 at its peak, and some have speculated that it could be an acquisition target. The company will report financial results for its most recent quarter next week and expects $975 million in revenue, lower than the $1.1 billion investors had hoped for earlier this year. It is not yet profitable.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Risher announced a handful of other changes on Thursday. He ended products focused on car rentals, as well as shared rides and luxury rides, and he promoted \uc2 Kristin Sverchek\uc2 , the head of business affairs, to president.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  also planned to tell employees that it would reduce their stock grants this year, according to a person familiar with the decision.\par \par \uc2 The return to office plan, Mr. Risher said, would require workers to come in Mondays, Wednesdays and Thursdays, with Tuesdays recommended, beginning after Labor Day. People will be allowed to work remotely for one month each year, and those living far from offices would not be required to come in.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Risher said he saw the moment as an opportunity to have a ''cultural reset, particularly around decision-making.''\par \par \uc2 He said that \uc2 Lyft\uc2  was successful with its early ride-hailing business, but that Mr. Green and Mr. Zimmer's idea to build a transportation network, with products focused on scooters, bikes, parking and rental cars, ''didn't really resonate with people.'' The company has pared back those offerings but does still have bikes and scooters.\par \par \uc2 ''So now, my focus is saying, 'Gosh, in ride share alone, there's an enormous amount of innovation left.' People desperately want to get out and live their lives, and we can help them,'' Mr. Risher said. ''And then maybe, over time, we can build some things back on top of that.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230429ej4t0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
